A||||11||lowfierogt||Who has a Sport Bike?||||5||
Z||000000||lowfierogt||09-24-2001||12:26 PM||||I bought a 2001 GSXR 600 and seems to have a little lifter nosie.   Is this normal after 1000 miles.   I had oil changed at 1700 miles and still makes the nosie.  when I had it in for service they said there was nothing wrong with it.   Does anyone with a sport bike here this noise?<BR> <A HREF="http://lowfierogt.no-ip.com" TARGET=_blank>http://lowfierogt.no-ip.com</A> <BR>86 1/2 GT<BR>85 GT<BR>87 coupe daily driver over 300k miles<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://lowfierogt.no-ip.com/87%20fiero_files/Jun0302.JPG"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Formula||09-24-2001||01:25 PM||||sohc motors usually make a lifter type noise.  I dont know what a bike uses though.<P>idk if that helps or not lol||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nashco||09-24-2001||02:13 PM||||I didn't think they had lifters...I thought they were a dohc setup...?  If the dealership says that's normal...you can probably trust them <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Most dealerships are pretty good for service, and if you're bike may need some work, they would definitely tell you...they obviously would want the business <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||09-24-2001||05:07 PM||||Most bikes (at least the ones I rode) don't have hydraulic lifters. There's normally a little bit of clearance there. Wouldn't worry about the clicking.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||EdsB52||09-25-2001||12:25 AM||||Older Gixxers had adjustable rockers (followers) and were very noisy even if recently adjusted. Since about the early 90's, Gixxers have solid shimms that adjust the clearance and hold the required clearance for 1,000's of miles. They are still inherently noisy engines, but are designed to run that way. You can have a shop check the clearances, but you'll probably find they're fine being a new bike.||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowfierogt||09-25-2001||09:53 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>Older Gixxers had adjustable rockers (followers) and were very noisy even if recently adjusted. Since about the early 90's, Gixxers have solid shimms that adjust the clearance and hold the required clearance for 1,000's of miles. They are still inherently noisy engines, but are designed to run that way. You can have a shop check the clearances, but you'll probably find they're fine being a new bike.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thanks for the info.   Im glad someone know about these engines.    I feel helpless.  I know alot about car engines but none about these new bike engines.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://lowfierogt.no-ip.com/87%20fiero_files/Jun0302.JPG">  <BR>http://lowfierogt.no-ip.com<BR>86 1/2 GT<BR>85 GT<BR>87 coupe daily driver over 300k miles||||reg||12
Z||000006||Boomtastic||09-27-2001||04:58 AM||||It's normal! My 2001 GSX-R 750 makes the same noise. I thought it was injectors though ..<P>------------------<BR>Boomtastic<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mirror_md_clr.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||EdsB52||09-27-2001||03:29 PM||||2001 Gixxer 750 (drewl), what a sweet bike. Actually my dream bike. Anything that winds out to 14 grand and can sustain that for long periods needs to be a little loose at idle. Wheely on bro.||||reg||1
Z||000008||lowfierogt||09-27-2001||04:51 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by EdsB52:<BR><B>2001 Gixxer 750 (drewl), what a sweet bike. Actually my dream bike. Anything that winds out to 14 grand and can sustain that for long periods needs to be a little loose at idle. Wheely on bro.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What wrong with the 600?<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://lowfierogt.no-ip.com/87%20fiero_files/Jun0302.JPG">  <BR>http://lowfierogt.no-ip.com<BR>86 1/2 GT<BR>85 GT<BR>87 coupe daily driver over 300k miles||||reg||2
Z||000009||EdsB52||09-27-2001||05:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowfierogt:<BR><B> What wrong with the 600?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Nothing at all. When they were first introduced in around 1991 or so, they were poop. Now they're every bit as good as the 750's, but smaller and a little less powerfull (not much). The problem is that I'm 6'1" and 215 lbs.  How can I squish onto that bike?! I have buddies that ride CBR600's and I got to drive one a while back, but I didn't like it. I felt too vertical, as opposed to the Gixxer750 where I become incorporated with the bike. Besides, you get used to the horsepower of any machine and want more after the initial rush wears off, so I would just want that feeling to last longer.||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowfierogt||09-27-2001||10:13 PM||||I understand totally.   Im 6'2"  220 and can't drive more then 50 miles.   Not a good long distance bike.   But is fast.   has 130 horses with EFI.   Only weighs 345, the 750 has 10 more horses and is 30 lbs more.  the 600 is taller then the 750.   I tested my friends 750 and raced it.   we where neck and neck the hole way down the drag strip.   the 750 and 600 are almost the same.   Now on the other hand the 1000 is alot different.  Not going to mess with a 1000.<P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://lowfierogt.no-ip.com/87%20fiero_files/Jun0302.JPG">  <BR>http://lowfierogt.no-ip.com<BR>86 1/2 GT<BR>85 GT<BR>87 coupe daily driver over 300k miles||||reg||2
Z||000011||artherd||09-29-2001||01:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowfierogt:<BR><B> 130 horses with EFI....   ...600 {cc}<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>WOW!  Now THAT's what I call specific output  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>More powerful than my brother's honda, that must really hall tail  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I want a 748(or 916/996) Ducati one of these days.  I don't need to go insanely fast, just being at one with the roads and the air and the trees and an Italian engine is fine with me  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
